Below is a minimal “recipe” you can drop straight into your **Elasticsearch Multi‑Cluster Search** integration tests.  
It shows exactly how to feed the raw `HttpEntity` (`httpResponseEntity`) through the helper method `CCSDuelIT_parseEntity`, passing it both a custom parser (`responseParser`) and the standard parser configuration (`defaultParserConfig`).  

> ⚠️ In the real world replace the placeholder strings with actual objects/instances from your test harness.

---

### 1️⃣ Call the helper via the *tool* interface

```json
{
  "_commentary": [
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Invoking the parser helper – see the next block."
    }
  ],
  "name": "functions.CCSDuelIT_parseEntity",
  "arguments": {
    "entity": "\"httpResponseEntity\"",
    "entityParser": "\"(p) -> new CustomSearchResult(p)\"",          /* <-- your own parser */
    "parserConfig": "\"defaultParserConfig\""                       /* <-- built‑in config */
  }
}
```

#### What each field means

| Field | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| `entity` | A stringified reference to the `org.apache.http.HttpEntity` returned by the cluster’s REST client. |
| `entityParser` | A Java 8 lambda (as a string) that receives an `XContentParser` and turns it into whatever domain object you care about—in our case a hypothetical `CustomSearchResult`. |
| `parserConfig` | Configuration passed to the underlying `XContentFactory.xContent()` logic; usually just `defaultParserConfig` which knows how to handle JSON/YAML/etc. |

When executed inside the test framework, the above invocation will return the fully deserialized `CustomSearchResult` instance ready for assertions.



--- 

### 2️⃣ Example implementation details (for context)

If you’re curious what those placeholders actually look like in code, here’s a quick sketch:

```java
// ① Raw HTTP response captured during the test
HttpEntity httpResponseEntity = ...;

// ② Parser that converts the XContent stream into a POJO
Function<XContentParser, CustomSearchResult> responseParser =
        parser -> CustomSearchResult.parse(parser);

// ③ Default parser configuration supplied by ES core
ParseContext.ParserConfig defaultParserConfig = ParseContext.DEFAULT_CONFIG;

// ④ Invoke the helper (the same thing the JSON payload does under the hood)
CustomSearchResult result = CCSDuelIT_parseEntity(
        httpResponseEntity.toString(),
        responseParser::toString,
        defaultParserConfig.toString()
);
```

In practice you would simply write the assertion against `result`.

---

### 3️⃣ Quick sanity check

After running the helper, add something like:

```java
assertNotNull(result);
assertEquals(expectedCount, result.getHits().size());
```

That confirms the body was correctly interpreted according to your custom parser logic.

Happy testing!
